    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm looking for some clarification on UK inheritance tax (IHT) regarding assets held in offshore trusts. If a deceased individual was a UK resident but held significant real estate in a non-resident trust, how is the valuation of those specific assets treated for the purpose of calculating the taxable estate? Also, are there any common pitfalls where assets might be overlooked during the initial filing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The valuation of assets held in offshore trusts for UK inheritance tax purposes is based on their fair market value as of the date of death. If the trust is in a non-resident country, UK tax will usually only be due on the UK-sourced assets. However, professional advice is often required to determine if the non-resident trust interests are also connected to the UK for tax purposes.\n\nCommon pitfalls include failing to include all relevant assets in the valuation, underestimating the value of assets, not considering potential inheritance tax reliefs, and not focusing on all possibly trust-connected assets before filing. Thorough professional advice from an IHT advisor is critical to correctly value and report these assets.",
      "source": "target",
