    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. Regarding those \"overlooked\" assets you mentioned—specifically when dealing with offshore structures—are there certain types of assets that tend to be more difficult to track down or that people often forget to disclose during the probate process? Like, things that aren't immediately obvious on a standard bank statement?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "One of the most challenging types of assets to track in cases involving offshore structures includes cash presences in banks, unless they are surface deposits – making it hard to ascertain the gross value on bills of lading or commercial invoices. People also often forget to disclose tangible personal property in transit (like motor vehicles or jewelry being shipped or stored across borders), which needs to be accounted for due to its financial value. Additionally, outright beneficial interests in offshore companies, dividends, and silent investments can be easily missed without specialized knowledge, especially when ownership stakes are unclear or held indirectly through third parties. Then there's the matter of intellectual property rights, which can have considerable value and may be registered in non-tax transparent jurisdictions. Accurate and exhaustive asset reporting requires expertise in both tax law and international financial transactions, and it's common to underestimate the implications of offshore trust arrangements and investments. Last but not least, digital assets such as cryptocurrencies also present challenges due to their novel and evolving legal status but can be significant in value. It's essential to consult with professionals who have experience in these fields to ensure all assets, no matter how unconventional, are accounted for during the probate process.",
      "source": "target",
